If Google posters and other Usenet influxes are people that type words
and sentences and write basically sensical things, we'll all be ok.
Your "traditions" are mostly pedantry.  Quality discussion is about
interesting ideas well-expressed, not formatting minutiae.  One
shouldn't compare r.g.r.n to a scholarly journal.  It's just plain
folks writing for fun.  In 17 years of Usenet I've never had any
significant problem reading or responding to a posting written in
basically good English.  Rearranging a few sections of text or
removing some extra spacing is part of the drill.  The concerns
underlying these "traditions" are born of an attitude that doesn't
tolerate deviation, and cement some kind of group identity for people
who communicate on newsgroups such as this one a lot, the exact
sociological purpose of which, I continue to maintain, will make an
interesting study.

